GUIDES=[
 ('packaging-quote-checklist','Sourcing guide','What do you need for a custom packaging quote?','Prepare a brief that lets suppliers quote the same specification.', [('Start with the product and quantity','Describe the product, packed weight or fill weight, approximate dimensions, and quantity per size and design. A total quantity without the number of artwork versions can hide important setup differences.'),('Define the package and artwork','Identify the box, bag, pouch, card or label format. Share approximate dimensions with units, print coverage, number of printed sides and finish preferences. If the format is undecided, describe the packing and delivery problem instead.'),('Clarify sampling and timing','State what the sample needs to verify: structure, fit, printed color, sealing or a specific finish. Include your launch window and delivery country. Production timing should be confirmed against the final specification and approvals; shipping is a separate part of the plan.'),('Compare quotations on the same basis','Ask suppliers to separate tooling or setup, samples, production, packing and freight where applicable. Confirm the quoted specification, quantity per design, delivery basis, payment conditions and quotation validity in writing.')]),
 ('mailer-boxes-vs-shipping-boxes','Structure guide','Mailer boxes vs shipping boxes: which fits your order?','Compare presentation, transit protection and packing workflow.', [('What is the difference?','Mailer boxes commonly use a folding lid and locking flaps for an opening experience suited to ecommerce presentation. Shipping boxes commonly use flaps closed with tape for transport. Either structure still needs the right board, dimensions and packing method for the contents.'),('Compare the product journey','A lightweight skincare set with an insert and a large heavy item create different loads. Review packed weight, internal movement, expected stacking and the actual courier route. Shape alone does not establish transport performance.'),('Review fit before decorative finishes','Begin with the product dimensions and the working space needed to place and remove it. Review internal dimensions with a structural sample; internal and external measurements are not interchangeable.'),('What should the sample confirm?','Check closure, insert retention, movement and ease of packing with the actual contents. Select any transport tests based on the agreed distribution conditions. A visual mockup cannot prove protection in transit.')]),
 ('stand-up-vs-flat-bottom-pouches','Pouch guide','Stand-up vs flat-bottom pouches: what should you compare?','Use filling, shelf presentation and protection needs to guide format choice.', [('Compare the format before choosing a finish','A stand-up pouch uses a bottom gusset to support the filled pack. A flat-bottom pouch has a defined base and side panels. Their suitability depends on filled-product behavior, target dimensions, closure and packing operations.'),('Material structure is a separate decision','Pouch shape does not specify oxygen or moisture barrier. State the product, expected shelf life, storage environment, distribution conditions and filling process so a material direction can be reviewed.'),('Confirm weight and dimensions with a filled sample','The same stated fill weight can occupy different volumes for whole beans, powder or pet treats. Compare physical fit, zipper clearance and seal area using the intended product. A stock bag may help screen shape but does not validate a final printed laminate.'),('Document the agreed specification','Record size convention, material layers, thickness, printing, zipper or valve, test needs and quantity per design. Any food-contact, recyclability or shelf-life claim needs evidence relevant to the selected structure and intended use.')])
]
